{"command": "cd /testbed && ls -la specs/001-transactions/ && echo === && cat specs/001-transactions/spec.md 2>/dev/null | head -100", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "total 56\ndrwxr-xr-x 4 bench bench  4096 Oct 17 17:39 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 bench bench  4096 Oct 17 17:16 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 bench bench  4096 Oct 17 17:25 checklists\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 bench bench  4096 Oct 17 17:39 contracts\n-rw-r--r-- 1 bench bench  3188 Oct 17 17:39 data-model.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 bench bench  6898 Oct 17 17:48 plan.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 bench bench  4725 Oct 17 17:39 quickstart.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 bench bench  6701 Oct 17 17:36 research.md\n-rw-r--r-- 1 bench bench 11596 Oct 17 17:24 spec.md\n===\n# Feature Specification: Atomic Batch Journal Writer (BatchWriter)\n\n**Feature Branch**: `[001-transactions]`\n\n**Created**: 2026-10-17\n\n**Status**: Draft\n\n**Input**: User description: \"tinydb milestone 1: Build tinydb.journal.BatchWriter(db), wrapping a supplied TinyDB instance without changing its ordinary APIs. Use the default table only. R01: apply(operations) accepts a list of operation dictionaries. Each operation is either {'op':'insert','document':dict} or {'op':'remove','doc_id':positive integer}. Return a list of inserted document IDs (removes add no result). Empty input returns []. Unknown/malformed operations raise ValueError. R02: Batch application is atomic: validate and apply sequentially, but on any error restore the complete pre-batch default-table data. A remove targeting a missing ID is an error. Previously acquired db.table('_default') handles must reflect both success and rollback. R03: Ordinary TinyDB reads/writes and preexisting documents continue to work. Inserted and returned nested data must not alias the caller's input. Mutating an operation after apply cannot mutate the database.\"\n\n## User Scenarios & Testing *(mandatory)*\n\n### User Story 1 - Apply a batch of insert/remove operations (Priority: P1)\n\nA caller holding a TinyDB instance wraps it with a BatchWriter and submits a\nbatch of operations as a list of operation dictionaries. Each operation either\ninserts a document (possibly with nested lists/dicts) into the default table or\nremoves an existing document by its positive integer document ID. The batch is\nvalidated and applied sequentially, and the caller receives the list of inserted\ndocument IDs in insertion order; remove operations contribute no result.\n\n**Why this priority**: This is the core capability the feature exists to\nprovide. Without it the feature has no value; with it alone the feature is a\nviable, testable MVP.\n\n**Independent Test**: Can be fully tested by creating a TinyDB instance, wrapping\nit with BatchWriter, calling apply() with a mixed insert/remove batch, and\nasserting the returned ID list and the resulting default-table contents.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** a default table containing preexisting documents, **When**\n   apply() is called with a non-empty list of valid insert and remove\n   operations, **Then** all operations are applied in order to the default\n   table and a list of the inserted document IDs (insertion order; removes add\n   nothing) is returned.\n2. **Given** a BatchWriter, **When** apply() is called with an empty list,\n   **Then** [] is returned and the default table is unchanged.\n3. **Given** a batch containing an unknown or malformed operation (op not\n   'insert'/'remove', missing or wrong-typed fields, non-dict operation,\n   non-list input, or a doc_id that is not a positive integer), **When**\n   apply() is called, **Then** a ValueError is raised and no partial changes\n   remain in the default table.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 2 - Atomic all-or-nothing batch (Priority: P2)\n\nA caller submits a batch that fails partway through (for example, a remove\ntargeting a missing document ID, or a malformed operation after several\nsuccessful ones). The whole batch is rolled back: the default table is restored\nto its complete pre-batch state, and previously acquired handles to the default\ntable observe the rollback. On success, those same handles observe the applied\nchanges.\n\n**Why this priority**: Atomicity is the safety property that makes batch\napplication trustworthy; it is the second pillar of the milestone after basic\napplication.\n\n**Independent Test**: Can be fully tested by seeding the default table, acquiring\na table handle, applying a batch that fails mid-way, and asserting the table\nhandle shows exactly the pre-batch data; then applying a valid batch and\nasserting the handle shows the updated data.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** a non-empty pre-batch default table and a batch whose later\n   operation targets a missing document ID, **When** apply() is called,\n   **Then** an error is raised and the default table is restored to its\n   complete pre-batch data (documents inserted earlier in the same batch are\n   removed, documents removed earlier in the same batch are restored).\n2. **Given** a previously acquired handle to the default table, **When** a\n   successful batch is applied, **Then** the handle reflects the applied\n   changes; when a failing batch is applied, **Then** the handle reflects the\n   rolled-back pre-batch data.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 3 - Non-invasive wrap with independent data copies (Priority: P3)\n\nA caller uses BatchWriter alongside ordinary TinyDB usage. Wrapping the\ninstance does not change ordinary TinyDB reads or writes, and preexisting\ndocuments remain accessible and mutable through the ordinary API. Data inserted\nvia a batch and data returned by apply() are independent copies: mutating the\ncaller's input documents or the returned IDs' associated data afterwards cannot\nmutate the database contents.\n\n**Why this priority**: Compatibility and data-isolation guarantees protect\nexisting consumers; they refine the feature rather than define its core value.\n\n**Independent Test**: Can be fully tested by inserting documents through the\nordinary API and through a batch, then mutating the caller's original input\ndictionaries and verifying the database contents are unchanged, and by\nperforming ordinary TinyDB reads/writes before and after batch use.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** a TinyDB instance with preexisting documents, **When** it is\n   wrapped with BatchWriter and ordinary TinyDB reads/writes are performed\n   before and after batch operations, **Then** all reads/writes behave exactly\n"}